.TH CCOL_LOG_TRACE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clog_trace \- emit a log line at TRACE level
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog_trace(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Captures __FILE__, __LINE__ and __func__ by itself and writes a log
line at CLOG_TRACE level.  No backtrace is appended.

The output format is:
  ts=<ISO-8601-UTC> level=TRACE proc=<name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>) src=<file>:<line> func=<fn> [fields] msg=<text>

The call does nothing when the current minimum level of the logger is
above CLOG_TRACE.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
TRACE is the finest level and makes a high volume of output, so turn
it on only to diagnose low-level behaviour.
Every log_* macro shares the same thread-safety guarantee: the shared
write lock protects the whole cycle that formats and writes a record,
so no partial line falls between the lines of another thread.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clog_trace(lg, "entered function, x=%d", x);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_debug (3),
.BR clog_info (3),
